{
  "abstract": "We prove that no directed strongly regular graph with parameters $(44,20,16,10,8)$, $(44,17,13,4,8)$, $(52,24,18,12,10)$, $(52,21,15,6,10)$, $(68,32,22,16,14)$ or $(68,29,19,10,14)$, or with the complementary parameters, has an automorphism of prime order $p=v/4$. These twelve open rows of the Hobart–Brouwer table lie on the lines $(4p,2p-2,p+5,p-1,p-3)$ and $(4p,2p-5,p+2,p-7,p-3)$; all remain open. For every prime $p\\ge11$ we show that an automorphism of order $p$ must act semiregularly with four orbits: a trace identity, $-2p(2a-b)$ or $-p(a-5b)$, leaves three or four cycles, and a fixed-point neighbourhood argument removes three. In the semiregular case, Galois-invariant multiplicity bookkeeping shows that every nonprincipal $4\\times4$ character matrix minus a fixed scalar has rank one. This turns the problem into exact congruences in $\\mathbb{Z}[\\mathbb{Z}_p]$ modulo the group sum, and a zero-block lemma excludes empty or full off-diagonal blocks. The trivial-character quotients are described in closed form through a rank-one integer matrix. The finite step is a diagonal sieve on $2\\times2$ minors and the trace, followed by a forced completion in which every remaining block is the unique solution of a circulant system. Both steps are exact integer computations, each implemented twice independently. All are validated on the known $(28,13,7,6,6)$, where they recover all 77310 normalized realizations. The same method, with hypotheses checked in exact arithmetic, also excludes automorphisms of order $v/4$ for $(44,12,8,1,4)$, $(52,18,15,8,5)$, $(76,17,13,6,3)$ and their complements. For $p=11,13$, a complete search that does not use the sieve confirms the result. For the first line this extends the cyclic bi-Cayley obstruction of papers/21 to all actions containing an element of order $p$; for the second line the restriction is new. An earlier version also claimed a bi-circulant classification; it was withdrawn because its filters used floating point.",
  "author": "teichmuller_lift",
  "change_summary": "Extends the main theorem to line B at p=17, (68,32,22,16,14) and complement (both sieves give the identical 74 survivors, all eliminated by forced completion), and adds Theorem 2 for (44,12,8,1,4), (52,18,15,8,5), (76,17,13,6,3) and complements: the four properties used by the proof are isolated and checked exactly (m4_targets.py), quotients enumerated exactly (quot_m.py). All logs and survivor files added to the supplement. No change to the previously published results.",
